THOUGHT: colorChannel (and the other channels) are annotated @Nullable in PointSpriteControllerRenderData, so NullAway complains when they’re dereferenced in flush(int[]). At runtime, these channels are allocated by the renderer before rendering; if any were actually null, rendering that controller’s particles would be impossible. A safe, localized fix is to skip processing a renderData entry when any required channel is null. This avoids changing annotations and doesn’t affect other errors. I’ll add a null-guard at the top of the inner loop’s per-data section, checking all four nullable channels and continuing if any is null, which will make NullAway see that colorChannel is non-null when used.

```bash
cd /home/vscode/nullness-benchmarks/libgdx && sed -i '155,167s/^      FloatChannel scaleChannel = data.scaleChannel;/      FloatChannel scaleChannel = data.scaleChannel;
      FloatChannel regionChannel = data.regionChannel;
      FloatChannel positionChannel = data.positionChannel;
      FloatChannel colorChannel = data.colorChannel;
      FloatChannel rotationChannel = data.rotationChannel;

      if (regionChannel == null || colorChannel == null || scaleChannel == null || rotationChannel == null) {
        continue;
      }/' gdx/src/com/badlogic/gdx/graphics/g3d/particles/batches/PointSpriteParticleBatch.java && ./gradlew gdx:compileJava -x test -q > new_build_output.log 2>&1 && grep -E "NullAway|error: \\[NullAway\\]" -n new_build_output.log | tail -n 20```

